24	/// Links to a URL or a location in the document.
25	///
26	/// By default, links do not look any different from normal text. However, you
27	/// can easily apply a style of your choice with a show rule.
28	///
29	/// = Example <example>
30	/// ```example
31	/// #show link: underline
32	///
33	/// https://example.com \
34	///
35	/// #link("https://example.com") \
36	/// #link("https://example.com")[
37	///   See example.com
38	/// ]
39	/// ```
40	///
41	/// = Syntax <syntax>
42	/// This function also has dedicated syntax: Text that starts with `http://` or
43	/// `https://` is automatically turned into a link.
44	///
45	/// = Hyphenation <hyphenation>
46	/// If you enable hyphenation or justification, by default, it will not apply to
47	/// links to prevent unwanted hyphenation in URLs. You can opt out of this
48	/// default via `{show link: set text(hyphenate: true)}`.
49	///
50	/// = Accessibility <accessibility>
51	/// The destination of a link should be clear from the link text itself, or at
52	/// least from the text immediately surrounding it. In PDF export, Typst will
53	/// automatically generate a tooltip description for links based on their
54	/// destination. For links to URLs, the URL itself will be used as the tooltip.
55	///
56	/// = Links in HTML export <links-in-html-export>
57	/// In @html[HTML export], a link to a @label[label] or @location[location] will
58	/// be turned into a fragment link to a named anchor point. To support this,
59	/// targets without an existing ID will automatically receive an ID in the DOM.
60	/// How this works varies by which kind of HTML node(s) the link target turned
61	/// into:
62	///
63	/// - If the link target turned into a single HTML element, that element will
64	///   receive the ID. This is, for instance, typically the case when linking to
65	///   a top-level heading (which turns into a single `<h2>` element).
66	///
67	/// - If the link target turned into a single text node, the node will be
68	///   wrapped in a `<span>`, which will then receive the ID.
69	///
70	/// - If the link target turned into multiple nodes, the first node will receive
71	///   the ID.
72	///
73	/// - If the link target turned into no nodes at all, an empty span will be
74	///   generated to serve as a link target.
75	///
76	/// If you rely on a specific DOM structure, you should ensure that the link
77	/// target turns into one or multiple elements, as the compiler makes no
78	/// guarantees on the precise segmentation of text into text nodes.
79	///
80	/// If present, the automatic ID generation tries to reuse the link target's
81	/// label to create a human-readable ID. A label can be reused if:
82	///
83	/// - All characters are alphabetic or numeric according to Unicode, or a
84	///   hyphen, or an underscore.
85	///
86	/// - The label does not start with a digit or hyphen.
87	///
88	/// These rules ensure that the label is both a valid CSS identifier and a valid
89	/// URL fragment for linking.
90	///
91	/// As IDs must be unique in the DOM, duplicate labels might need disambiguation
92	/// when reusing them as IDs. The precise rules for this are as follows:
93	///
94	/// - If a label can be reused and is unique in the document, it will directly
95	///   be used as the ID.
96	///
97	/// - If it's reusable, but not unique, a suffix consisting of a hyphen and an
98	///   integer will be added. For instance, if the label `<mylabel>` exists
99	///   twice, it would turn into `mylabel-1` and `mylabel-2`.
100	///
101	/// - Otherwise, a unique ID of the form `loc-` followed by an integer will be
102	///   generated.
103	///
104	/// = Links in bundle export <links-in-bundle-export>
105	/// In @reference:bundle[bundle export], linking still works as usual. For
106	/// instance, if you attach a label to an element in one document, links in
107	/// other documents can reference that label. In addition, documents and assets
108	/// are also directly linkable. To link to a full document or asset, you can
109	/// attach a label to it or @query[query] for it and extract its
110	/// @location[location].
111	///
112	/// ```typ
113	/// #document("index.html")[
114	///   // Link to document.
115	///   #link(<appendix>)[To appendix]
116	///
117	///   // Link into document.
118	///   See the #link(<glossary>)[Glossary]
119	///   for more information.
120	/// ]
121	///
122	/// #document("appendix.html")[
123	///   = Definitions
124	///   ...
125	///
126	///   = Glossary <glossary>
127	///   ...
128	/// ] <appendix>
129	/// ```
130	///
131	/// Cross-document links are emitted as relative paths (potentially with
132	/// fragments). Typst automatically assigns anchor names per document based on
133	/// the same rules as in HTML export. In HTML and SVG documents, these are
134	/// emitted as `id` attributes on elements. In PDF documents, they are emitted
135	/// as _named destinations._ PNG documents do not support linking.
136	///
137	/// Note that links always use full relative paths. In some scenarios (primarily
138	/// for multi-page websites), this may not be desirable. For instance, you may
139	/// want to generate a `/blog/index.html` document while wanting to link to it
140	/// as just `/blog`. Furthermore, your web server might treat `/blog` and
141	/// `/blog/` as interchangeable and serve `/blog/index.html` for both. If a user
142	/// then navigates to `/blog`, relative links to other pages generated by Typst
143	/// will no longer work. Currently, Typst does not have a way to directly hook
144	/// into the built-in link handling. That said, in HTML export, depending on
145	/// your use case, it may be possible to adjust the built-in link handling with
146	/// a show rule on `{html.elem.where(tag: "a")}`.
147	#[elem(since = "forever", Locatable)]
148	pub struct LinkElem {
149	    /// The destination the link points to.
150	    ///
151	    /// - To link to web pages, `dest` should be a valid URL string. If the URL
152	    ///   is in the `mailto:` or `tel:` scheme and the `body` parameter is
153	    ///   omitted, the email address or phone number will be the link's body,
154	    ///   without the scheme.
155	    ///
156	    /// - To link to another part of the document, `dest` can take one of three
157	    ///   forms:
158	    ///   - A @label[label] attached to an element. If you also want automatic
159	    ///     text for the link based on the element, consider using a
160	    ///     @ref[reference] instead.
161	    ///
162	    ///   - A @location (typically retrieved from @here, @locate or @query).
163	    ///
164	    ///   - A dictionary with a `page` key of type @int[integer] and `x` and `y`
165	    ///     coordinates of type @length[length]. Pages are counted from one, and
166	    ///     the coordinates are relative to the page's top left corner.
167	    ///
168	    /// ```example
169	    /// = Introduction <intro>
170	    /// #link("mailto:hello@typst.app") \
171	    /// #link(<intro>)[Go to intro] \
172	    /// #link((page: 1, x: 0pt, y: 0pt))[
173	    ///   Go to top
174	    /// ]
175	    /// ```
176	    #[required]
177	    #[parse(
178	        let dest = args.expect::<LinkTarget>("destination")?;
179	        dest.clone()
180	    )]
181	    pub dest: LinkTarget,
182	
183	    /// The content that should become a link.
184	    ///
185	    /// If `dest` is an URL string, the parameter can be omitted. In this case,
186	    /// the URL will be shown as the link.
187	    #[required]
188	    #[parse(match &dest {
189	        LinkTarget::Dest(Destination::Url(url)) => match args.eat()? {
190	            Some(body) => body,
191	            None => body_from_url(url),
192	        },
193	        _ => args.expect("body")?,
194	    })]
195	    pub body: Content,
196	
197	    /// A destination style that should be applied to elements.
198	    #[internal]
199	    #[ghost]
200	    pub current: Option<Destination>,
201	}
