### HTML create_link_anchors (html link.rs:26-45)
```rust
pub fn create_link_anchors(
    document: &mut HtmlDocument,
    targets: &FxHashSet<Location>,
) -> FxHashMap<Location, EcoString> {
    if targets.is_empty() {
        return FxHashMap::default();
    }
    let mut work = Work::new();
    let introspector = Arc::clone(document.introspector());
    traverse(
        &mut work,
        targets,
        &mut AnchorGenerator::new(introspector.as_ref()),
        &mut document.root_mut().children,
    );
    work.ids
}
```
- Mutates DOM in place: traverses the node tree, and when it encounters a node whose location is in `targets`, assigns an id attribute (or wraps text in a span, or inserts an empty span).
- Uses `AnchorGenerator` to produce human-readable ids (reuse label if possible).
- Returns loc→id map.
- The traverse logic with queue/drain handles the segmentation cases described in the LinkElem doc (single element / text node / multiple nodes / no nodes).

### create_paged_link_anchors (bundle link.rs:69-88)
```rust
fn create_paged_link_anchors(
    doc: &PagedDocument,
    targets: &FxHashSet<Location>,
) -> Vec<(Location, EcoString)> {
    let elements = doc.introspector().elements();
    let mut generator = AnchorGenerator::new(doc.introspector().as_ref());
    let mut anchors = Vec::new();
    let mut targets: Vec<_> = targets.iter().copied().collect();
    targets.sort_by_key(|loc| elements.loc_index(loc));

    for target in targets {
        if let Some(elem) = elements.get_by_loc(&target) {
            let anchor = generator.identify(elem.label());
            anchors.push((target, anchor));
        }
    }
    anchors
}
```
Key points:
- Sorts targets by `loc_index` (document order) before assigning anchors.
- Why sort? Because `AnchorGenerator::identify` is stateful (it uses counters for disambiguation: `loc_counter`, `label_counter`). The disambiguation suffix (`-1`, `-2`) depends on the order in which labels are encountered. To make anchor assignment deterministic across runs (and stable across introspection iterations / memoization), targets must be processed in a fixed order — document order. If processed in arbitrary (e.g. HashSet) order, the same `<mylabel>` appearing twice might get `mylabel-1`/`mylabel-2` swapped between runs, breaking link stability and memoization correctness. Also, `disambiguate` checks for collisions against existing labels via `introspector.label_count`, so deterministic order ensures deterministic IDs.

